 are usually bright yellow, but dark yellow-orange and pale varieties occur in nature and in cultivation.","image":"","commonName":"Denison's vanda","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":409708,"botanicalName":"<em>Vanda</em> <em>dearei</em>","entityDescription":"This hot-growing species has been a break-through in breeding many modern yellow hybrids, notably in 1950s and 60s. Its progeny carries bright yellow colour, longevity and sweet fragrance of their flowers. However, due to hot-growing, natural environment, its hybrids are less cold-tolerant that the blue-flowered species.","image":"","commonName":"Deare's vanda","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":18758,"botanicalName":"<em>Vanda</em> <em>cristata</em>","entityDescription":"A small-sized, evergreen, tropical orchid species. As a monopodial orchids, the new growth emerges from the tip of a long central rhizome. V-shaped, linear leaves angle out from the main stem, and as the new ones grow from the top, old, lower leaves gradually fall off. New roots grow straight out of the main stem, following the acsending growing trend. Short inflorescences, carrying 2-6 flowers appear from the leaf axils in spring and summer. Flower are waxy, thick-textured, yellow-green. Characteristic for this species are two distinct horns on the tip of the white flower lip covered with dark red markings. Each flower is 2.5 - 5cm across.","image":"","commonName":"comb vanda","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":409706,"botanicalName":"<em>Vanda</em> <em>concolor</em>","entityDescription":"A small-sized, evergreen, epiphytic orchid species. As a typical example of monopodial orchid, 